# React component discipline

Match existing component patterns — functional vs class, file naming, colocation,
and styling — before adding or editing UI code.

Keep components focused. Extract subcomponents or hooks when logic grows, using
the same folder and naming conventions the project already follows.

Follow the Rules of Hooks: only call hooks at the top level of React functions
or custom hooks, never inside loops, conditions, or nested functions.

Prefer existing design-system components and tokens over one-off styles. Do not
duplicate primitives the codebase already exports.

Avoid unnecessary re-renders: reuse established state-management patterns
(Context, Redux, React Query, or local state) instead of introducing a parallel
approach.

Do not fetch data inside render. Use the project's existing data-loading hooks,
loaders, or server-component patterns.
